Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Bedrock expands API support and introduces Cross Region Inferencing for OpenAI models - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock has expanded its API support to include OpenAI GPT-5.6 models (Sol, Terra, and Luna) on the bedrock-runtime endpoint, with compatibility for the Responses, Converse, and Chat Completions APIs. The update introduces cross-region inference, allowing customers to access higher throughput and lower inference costs by automatically routing requests across multiple AWS Regions. Geo cross-region inference keeps data processed within a specific geography, while Global cross-region inference serves requests from any commercial AWS Region where the model is available. This launch also expands API support for OpenAI models, enabling usage of the Responses API, Chat Completions API, and Converse API on the bedrock-runtime endpoint. Cross-region inference for OpenAI models is now available in all AWS Regions where OpenAI models on Amazon Bedrock are offered. Usage is logged and can be attributed to model invocation logging, Amazon CloudWatch metrics, AWS Cost Explorer, and the AWS Cost and Usage Report. Cross-region inference for OpenAI models is available in all AWS Regions where OpenAI models on Amazon Bedrock are offered. To get started, review the model cards for GPT 5.6 (Sol, Tera, and Luna) in the Amazon Bedrock User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/amazon-bedrock-cross-region-openai-v2/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"caching-for-llm-inference","skill_description":"Sets up a shared L2 KV cache for self-hosted LLM inference on Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey, so GPU servers load prefill KV state over EFA RDMA instead of recomputing it. Checks fit, estimates savings with KVTrace, builds a business case, creates the cache, connects vLLM or SGLang (also as NVIDIA Dynamo or llm-d workers) through an LMCache server, tunes for cost or latency, and troubleshoots. Use when a builder serves open-weight models on GPUs (for example H100 or B200, on Amazon EC2 or Amazon EKS) for multi-turn chat, agents, RAG, or long contexts and asks about KV cache offloading, prefix cache sharing, prompt caching for self-hosted models, LMCache with Valkey, or lower TTFT or GPU cost. Not for Bedrock prompt caching, LLM response or semantic caching, or general application caching.\n\nServices: elasticache, ec2\nTasks: deploy, optimize, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, data-scientist, devops\nWorkload: ai-ml","skill_name":"caching-for-llm-inference"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-ai-ml","skill_description":"Selects, deploys, and customizes AI models on Amazon SageMaker. Training or Processing jobs, fine-tuning (SFT/DPO/RLVR/RLAIF), model selection, evaluation, SageMaker or Bedrock deployment, inference optimization and endpoint diagnostics. Use when fine-tuning models on SageMaker, choosing a base model to customize or deploy from SageMaker JumpStart or Hub, SageMakerPublicHub, or the model catalog, checking data quality (duplicates, languages, PII), transforming or validating data (reformat, clean, filter, dedupe, split, merge, redact), generating synthetic data, labeling data with a model, human review or annotation and applying its corrections, evaluating model quality, deploying or optimizing endpoints, configuring IAM/S3, or managing SageMaker Managed MLflow. Use for endpoint health, latency, logs, metrics, errors. Covers Serverless Model Customization, Nova and OSS deployment paths, and PySDK v3. NOT for SageMaker Ground Truth labeling jobs, Feature Store, or general AWS infrastructure.\n\nServices: sagemaker, s3, iam, bedrock\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, data-scientist\nWorkload: ai-ml","skill_name":"aws-ai-ml"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up Bedrock cross-region inference documentation"}
